  "Question": "What is \\[10 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{5} + \\dfrac{1}{10}\\right)^{-1}?\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(3\\)",
    "B": "\\(8\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{25}{2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{170}{3}\\)",
    "E": "\\(170\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "First evaluate the quantity inside the parentheses using a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac15+\\frac1{10}\n=\\frac5{10}+\\frac2{10}+\\frac1{10}\n=\\frac45.\n\\]\nAn exponent of \\(-1\\) means the reciprocal of a nonzero number. Since \\(\\frac45\\ne0\\), the expression equals\n\\[\n10\\left(\\frac45\\right)^{-1}\n=10\\cdot\\frac54\n=\\boxed{\\frac{25}{2}},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
